  "result": "I added `top_words(text, n)` for issue #12 and opened a PR: https://github.com/acme/textstats/pull/7. All 9 tests pass (`python -m unittest discover tests`).\n\n**The issue** asked for a function in `textstats/__init__.py` that returns the most frequent words as `(word, count)` tuples, at most `n` of them. It should ignore case and punctuation, break ties alphabetically, return `[]` for empty text, and have unit tests. A reviewer comment asked to use only the standard library.\n\n**How it works:**\n- It lowercases the text and splits it on whitespace.\n- It removes punctuation from the start and end of each word, so `hello,` and `hello!` both count as `hello`. Apostrophes inside a word stay, so `don't` is one word.\n- It counts the words with `collections.Counter`, sorts by count (highest first) and then alphabetically, and returns the first `n`.\n- If `n` is 0 or negative, it returns `[]`.\n\n**Tests:** I added 6 tests in `tests/test_textstats.py`, one for each requirement, plus checks for text that is only punctuation and for `n = 0`.\n\n**Git:** the commit is `7717e41` on the new branch `feature/top-words-12`, and its message includes \"Closes #12\". I fetched the issue and opened the PR through the ticket-manager and pr-opener agents. The change was small, so I planned and wrote it myself.",
